  <reporting-org ref="XI-IATI-CWSEC" secondary-reporter="0" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Commonwealth Secretariat</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Creating Shared Prosperity Through Intra Commonwealth Trade</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">With a renewed focus on member-led initiatives and closer collaboration with Commonwealth accredited organisations to achieve the goal of increased cooperation on intra-Commonwealth trade, the project seeks to strengthen intra-Commonwealth trade through the reduction of tariff and non-tariff barriers and build the capacity of member states&#x2014;particularly small states, least developed countries, and other vulnerable economies&#x2014;to expand trade and attract greater investment for advancing their economic development and enhancing their resilience to current and future global crises and external shocks. This will be done through 4 key intervention pathways: 1) Consensus building, and member-led initiatives; 2) Research and Policy Advisory, Advocacy; 3) Technical assistance and capacity building, including through the Commonwealth Small States Office; and 4) Commonwealth Trade Ministers Meeting. These four impact pathways will contribute to the building of economic resilience in all member states. The Commonwealth Secretariat is uniquely positioned to address Commonwealth specific trade promotion challenges and carry out Commonwealth specific trade research and policy advocacy. A strengthened advocacy strategy will support the embedding of research findings to underpin collaborative measures to identify and reduce barriers to intra commonwealth trade. The Commonwealth Trade ministers have endorsed a workplan to further the goal agreed by Commonwealth Heads of Government to increased intra-commonwealth trade to USD 2 trillion by 2030 and this goal can only be achieved through commonwealth led interventions. The CSSO trade advisory support for small states in Geneva is a unique program of support for Commonwealth member states not replicated by other multilateral organisations.</narrative>
  </description>
